  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a dopant strategy for a Ni-rich layered cathode, using paired Na⁺ and Ti⁴⁺ doping to improve performance under a very high charging voltage of 4.8 V. On the left, the particle cross-section suggests that Ti is concentrated to a supersaturated level near the surface, while the bulk remains the main Ni-rich layered material. This design is presented as a surface-focused modification rather than a uniform change throughout the particle.\n\nThe central schematic shows the layered crystal structure, with alternating Li and transition-metal layers. In the modified surface region, Na and Ti ions are incorporated, creating what appears to be a lattice-expanded, cation-densified surface. This is associated with structure stabilization: the figure explicitly links the strategy to suppressed microcracking and maintenance of the layered structure during high-voltage operation.\n\nOn the right, the same surface modification is shown to stabilize the interface. A thin cathode–electrolyte interphase is indicated, along with faster Li⁺ transport. At the same time, the doped surface blocks two common degradation pathways: transition-metal dissolution and oxygen release. Overall, the image conveys that Na/Ti co-doping strengthens both the internal structure and the surface/interface of the cathode at extreme charge conditions.",
